contentText:
The first official meeting of the Pauls was about as lovely as you&rsquo;d expect. Over the weekend, Paul McCartney sat down to talk about his upcoming solo album, The Boys of Dungeon Lane , with actor Paul Mescal, who is slated to portray the former Beatle in director Sam Mendes&rsquo; The Beatles: A Four-Film Cinematic Event , due out in April 2028.

The two men sat down for The Boys of Dungeon Lane: In Conversation with Paul McCartney & Paul Mescal , which debuted on Monday (May 25) on Amazon Live and the Amazon Music app. The 11-minute chat in a restaurant opens with a seemingly nervous Mescal asking, &ldquo;How do you feel about being interviewed?,&rdquo; with veteran interviewee McCartney smiling back, &ldquo;It depends if I like the person. Which is where we&rsquo;re running into a problem already &hellip; No, I find if I like who I&rsquo;m being interviewed by it comes easy.&rdquo;

The intimate conversation finds Mescal diving right into the personal nature of the songwriting on McCartney&rsquo;s album (due out on May 29), asking how the legendary pop songwriter is able to spin his memories into new music while also making them feel like they&rsquo;re in the &ldquo;present tense.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know how I do it,&rdquo; says McCartney during the interview, which is broken up with glimpses of archival pictures of the Beatles and young people laughing at the tables around them. &ldquo;I haven&rsquo;t got a formula. They used to ask me and John [Lennon], &lsquo;How&rsquo;d you do it? Who writes the music, who writes the words?&rsquo; I don&rsquo;t know. To me, I think any story or song you&rsquo;re gonna do, it&rsquo;s gotta involve memory. With the Beatles, we always tried to write something different.&rdquo;

McCartney says he likes writing about Lennon and late Beatles guitarist/songwriter George Harrison these days, because it&rsquo;s like &ldquo;revisiting them.&rdquo; He does just that on the new song &ldquo;Down South,&rdquo; which harkens back to his and Lennon&rsquo;s pre-fame days as children.

They also discuss the new album&rsquo;s collaboration with former bandmate Ringo Starr on the single &ldquo;Home To Us,&rdquo; as well as &ldquo; Days We Left Behind ,&rdquo; a romantic tune looking back at what Mescal dubs McCartney&rsquo;s &ldquo;full, brilliant, complicated&rdquo; relationship with his songwriting partner.

&ldquo;Looking back on your life, I ran into this guy called John Lennon, and he was fighting life &mdash; he had a lot trouble, his dad had left home, his mom had got run over, he had a lot of trouble in there &mdash; so he was putting up a shield, so he was very witty, very biting,&rdquo; McCartney says of Lennon. &ldquo;When it came to writing, that kind of relationship stayed there, so on this record, I might even refer to him in my mind, as if we&rsquo;re still writing together.&rdquo;

They also discuss an emotional track written in honor of McCartney&rsquo;s parents, &ldquo;Salesman Saint.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I often remember that my mom and dad had me in World War II. I&rsquo;ve always known that growing up, but at certain point you go &lsquo;Wow.&rsquo; McCartney says of the track on which he paints a picture of his cotton salesman father and nurse/midwife mother, who in the song becomes a saint. &ldquo;It occurred to me that it&rsquo;d be good to just put down some stuff about them carrying on through whatever they had to put up with,&rdquo; he says.

